    "final_text": "I fixed the bug where `buildUrl` dropped `retry=0` and `include_archived=false`. The full suite (`npm test`) passes, 9 of 9, including the two new regression tests.\n\n**Root cause:** In `src/url-builder.js`, `serializeQuery` skipped any value that counts as false in JavaScript (`if (!value) continue;`). So `0`, `false` and `''` were treated as missing. `appendQueryValue` did the same for array items, so `attempt: [0, 1]` came out as `attempt=1`. I reproduced both before changing anything.\n\n**Fix (2 lines):** Both checks now skip only `undefined` and `null`, which is the same check `normalizePath` already uses. The issue's example now produces `\u2026/webhooks/deliveries?dry_run=true&include_archived=false&retry=0`. The builder's API is unchanged.\n\n**Regression tests** in `tests/url-builder.test.js`:\n- `buildUrl keeps zero and false query values (ISSUE-214)`: the reported case, run through `buildUrl`.\n- `serializeQuery keeps falsy array items and empty strings but skips null and undefined`: the array case, and a check that `null`/`undefined` are still left out.\n\nBoth tests fail on the old code. In a scratch copy I also deleted the check entirely, and the second test caught `null` being written out as `attempt=null`.\n\n`ISSUE.md` now ends with a `Resolution` section naming the code path, the fix and both tests.\n\n**Decision for you:** an empty string now produces `key=` instead of being dropped. This follows from making only `null`/`undefined` mean \"leave this key out\", and it matches how `URLSearchParams` behaves. Any caller that relied on `''` disappearing will see the key appear; passing `undefined` instead keeps it out. If you'd rather keep dropping `''`, it's one extra condition in each check. I noted this in `ISSUE.md`.\n\n**What I didn't do:**\n- I didn't commit anything; the changes are in the working tree on `main`.\n- I ran `npm test` directly because the `dx run-gate` command from `CLAUDE.md` isn't installed here.\n- There's no lint or type-check setup in the project, so neither was run.",
